<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Troodos Mountains</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Troodos Mountains are the largest mountain range of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, in the west and centre of the island, rising to Mount Olympus at 1,952 metres. Their geology, a slice of ancient ocean floor, made them rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, which was mined from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and made Cyprus a centre of the metals trade. Their painted Byzantine churches are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/troodos-mountains.jpg" alt="Troodos Mountains" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chneophytou · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chionistra_winter_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Troodos Mountains are the largest mountain range of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, lying roughly in the centre of the island and covering a third of its area, across most of its western side. The highest peak is Mount Olympus, also called Hionistra, at 1,952 metres. The range forms a significant rain shadow on the island. The name probably comes from one of two sources: the Greek words for three and road, referring to the three roads that lead to the mountain, or a phrase meaning the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adonis">Adonis</a>. The Cyprus cedar and many other plants and animals found nowhere else live there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The mountains have been known since antiquity for their mines, which for centuries supplied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> to the entire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. In the Byzantine period they became a centre of Byzantine art, as churches and monasteries were built in the mountains, away from the threatened coastline. The region is known for Byzantine churches and monasteries richly decorated with murals, of which the Kykkos monastery is the richest. Nine churches and one monastery in Troodos together form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, first inscribed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1985; they include Panagia tou Araka, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agios-nikolaos">Agios Nikolaos</a> tis Stegis and Agios Ioannis Lampadistis at Kalopanagiotis.</p><p>Geologists value the range for an undisturbed ophiolite sequence, the Troodos Ophiolite. It rose slowly from the sea as the African and European plates collided, a process that eventually formed the island. Erosion exposed the former magma chamber beneath the mountains, revealing intact rocks and petrified pillow lava formed millions of years ago. Observations of the Troodos ophiolite by Ian Graham Gass and his colleagues were among the evidence that led to the theory of seafloor spreading.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The mountains hold resorts, villages in the valleys and on terraced hillsides, and many Byzantine monasteries and churches on the peaks. The climate is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, with greater daily ranges in temperature than on the coast. Trees include the Turkish pine and golden oak, with Anatolian black pine higher up. The endangered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> mouflon is the chief wild animal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Troodos Mountains” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Idalion grew on the hills of central Cyprus, near the copper-bearing foothills of <b>Troodos</b>, in the early Iron Age.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-idalion">Kingdom of Idalion</a></li><li><blockquote>Tamassos lay in the Pediaios valley at the foot of the copper-rich <b>Troodos</b> mountains and was settled from the Bronze Age.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tamassos">Kingdom of Tamassos</a></li></ul></section>
